sweet-touch-irq0 (lowclk 2026-11-14; design: docs/research/155-touch-ftm5-interrupt.md section 6 "Stage 0" and section 4 item 4): FTM5 touch interrupt STAGE 0 ONLY, read-only measurements behind boot-arg sweettouchirq=2. NO HARDWARE WRITE: this patch adds no store to any register (not pad CON/DAT, ECON, EFLTCON, EMASK, EPEND), no GIC call, no SPI attach, no handler. The host test (tools/test_touch_irq0_core.py) runs the core against a fake bus whose write slot fails the test when called and whose reads are checked against an independent offset list.
Sources [read]: research 155 with its citations: Google kernel/gs gs101-common-touch.dtsi:12 (TS_INT = gpa9[0]), pinctrl-gs.c:79 (gpa9 bank: pad base 0xC0, eint_offset 0x18, fltcon_offset 0x28), pinctrl-exynos.h:21,27-29 (EFLTCON 0x800, ECON 0x700, EMASK 0x900, EPEND 0xA00); board.json touch_pin_irq = gpa9[0], soc.json gpa9_bank 0x174D00C0.
sweettouchirq absent or 0 = behaviour unchanged: no mapping, no register read, no log line, the poll loop unchanged (every added call is behind the stage-0-on flag; the only added statements that run with it off are the flag tests). sweettouchirq=2 = stage 0. sweettouchirq=1 or any other value = `TOUCH GAP: sweettouchirq=%d: stage 1 not in this patch ...`, treated as 0.
Stage 0 [reads only], from the touch thread, nothing from an interrupt: (a) once, right after tg_cmu_dump (before any write of the driver): map the alive page (ml_io_map of 0x174D0000, the page the irq pad already lives in) and READ pad CON (0x174D00C0), pad DAT (+0xC4), ECON (0x174D0718), EFLTCON (0x174D0828), EMASK (0x174D0918), EPEND (0x174D0A18); one line `SWEET TOUCH IRQ0: gpa9 read once: econ %#x [pin0 %u] efltcon %#x [byte0 %#x] emask %#x [bit0 %u] epend %#x [bit0 %u] con %#x [func %#x] dat %#x [pin0 %u]`. (b) tg_reset_and_ready: one DAT read right BEFORE each FIFO read; `SWEET TOUCH IRQ0: reset-wait i=%u dat=%d ev=%02x` for the first 40 iterations, `SWEET TOUCH IRQ0: reset-wait first DAT change %d -> %d, %u us after reset release (i=%u)`, and one `SWEET TOUCH IRQ0: reset-wait end: %u samples, dat first %d last %d, DAT changed|never changed, controller-ready seen|not seen` per reset. (c) main poll loop: one DAT read before each tg_drain; 2x2 counters {DAT low, high} x {events found, empty}, DAT edges, longest run of low-with-empty; one line at most every 10 s: `SWEET TOUCH IRQ0: passes %u low/found %u low/empty %u high/found %u high/empty %u edges %u maxrun_low_empty %u drain_err %u verdict LOW-confirmed|NOT-confirmed|pending`. Verdict (research 155 s.6 item 4): (low,found)+(high,empty) >= 95 % of passes, only from 100 passes on (the 100 is a GUESS); GAP lines: (low,empty) over 20 % of passes, and the transition to NOT-confirmed (do not enable stage 1). The poll timing (1 ms with events, 8 ms without) is unchanged; the added work is one MMIO read per pass.
Failure handling [rule 7]: mapping failure, the irq pad not being gpa9[0] (bank 0x174D00C0 pin 0), a refused or failed read, all four EINT words reading 0xffffffff, a DAT word of 0xffffffff = a `TOUCH GAP: sweettouchirq=2: ...` line, stage 0 off, the poll continues. Whether the EINT words answer from XNU at all is research 155 U3: a bus fault (SError) on such a read would not be caught here.
Judge: every access of the new code goes through sti_rd of bsd/dev/sweet_touch_irq0.h: one allowlist (sti_rows[], six offsets, word aligned); there is no write row and no write function; the ops table has a wr slot that the kernel glue leaves NULL and the core never calls.
Files: new bsd/dev/sweet_touch_irq0.h (pure core between STI_CORE_BEGIN/END), bsd/dev/sweet_touch_gs101.c (boot-arg, mapping of the alive page, ops, the call sites). The sweettouchpoke allowlist is NOT changed. Diffed against the fully applied tree. ORDER: after 0354 (0348 is the last toucher of sweet_touch_gs101.c; 0354 does not touch it).
NOT RUN ON HARDWARE, not reviewed. It has no new write, so by AGENTS.md rule 8 it needs no Opus review; the read of the EINT words (U3) is the one untested access. Stage 1 (CON/ECON/EMASK/EPEND writes, GIC attach of INTID 67) is NOT in this patch and needs its own rule-8 review.
